// THE `&mut self` RECEIVER SPELLED `AddrOfTemp` WAS CHECKED AND NEVER RECORDED
// — the second half of B94, and the missing half was the DEPOSIT. `visit_place_
// base` runs visit()'s AddrOfTemp arm, which checks the receiver against every
// existing loan (including `mut_reservations`, D-d.2) and, per B94, records
// nothing. So NOTHING held `s` while `visit_args` evaluated the sibling
// operand, and `s.set(s.set(1))` — two `&mut self` on one root, upstream E0499
// — compiled. The MethodCall arm now deposits a B82 RESERVATION on a whole-root
// AddrOfTemp receiver, and the inner call's own auto-ref reaches the check arm
// that already consults it.
//
// ⚠ THE DEPOSIT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT GO THROUGH `take_borrow`, and that is
// what took three prices to learn. Routed through `record_borrow` the identical
// rule was CEILING 5 / COST 11 (`recvresvamut`) and CEILING 4 / COST 9
// (`recvamutarg`) and was declined twice; every one of those costs was
// take_borrow's BINDING-MUT question asked a SECOND time about a receiver
// reached through a `&mut` reference binding, which this arm has already asked
// and exempted by `root_is_ref`. Record-only it is CEILING 3 / COST 0.
// TWIN: pass/bc_recv_addroftemp_resv_admit — nineteen legal shapes, all proven
// to reach the deposit, including the `&mut`-binding receiver (9-11) and the
// field-path receiver the `rrp.path.empty()` guard keeps out (16-17).
package bc_recv_addroftemp_resv_fail;
struct S { a: i64 }
impl S { fn set(self: &mut S, k: i64) -> i64 { self.a = k; return k; } }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut s: S = S { a: 0i64 };
    return s.set(s.set(1i64)) as i32;   // E0499: two &mut self on one root
}
